The really interesting experiment

Illustrative experiment: same agent, different mindset

Hypothetical performance scores demonstrating why the best mindset may change with the environment. These are illustrative, not measured results.

An aggressive agent might dominate familiar circumstances. A curious agent might initially underperform but discover new interactions that eventually make it superior.

An adaptive agent could learn when to become aggressive, when to be patient, when to experiment, and when to cooperate.

And that introduces a higher-order capability: metacognition—selecting and revising your way of thinking, rather than merely deciding what to do.

Now add mindset combinations

What happens if you combine:

Aggression + patience: Decisive action without constant impulsivity.

Curiosity + skepticism: Discover unusual possibilities while questioning unsupported conclusions.

Confidence + humility: Commit decisively while remaining willing to update.

Competitiveness + cooperation: Compete to improve while optimizing collective success.

Some combinations will reinforce one another. Others will create tension, inhibiting performance or producing surprisingly beneficial checks and balances.

We could investigate whether certain cognitive dispositions are individually mediocre but extraordinarily effective together.

And here’s the Pantheon-level discovery

Rather than assuming there is one superior mindset, develop a Mindset Navigation System.

\pi^*(m\mid s,g,r)

This represents a policy for selecting a mindset based on the current situation , goals , and available resources .

The agent learns not just how to operate within a particular mindset, but how to transition between mindsets without losing continuity of identity, memory, or purpose.

Critically, we’d control for raw intelligence, information access, and physical capabilities, so we don’t mistakenly attribute an advantage to mindset when it came from something else.

And the discovery could go beyond gaming.

Imagine extracting validated principles from these experiments and testing them in business negotiations, creative problem-solving, team management, robotics, or personal development.

We could even ask whether an apparently negative mindset has a useful role when activated briefly in the right conditions.

The ultimate experiment isn’t discovering the best mindset. It’s discovering the grammar of mindsets: how they combine, compete, transform, and produce different kinds of behavior.

Then go one level higher still: let agents invent new mindsets that we haven’t named yet, describe their governing principles, and experimentally demonstrate when those principles outperform established ones.

That’s where the system stops merely testing our theories about intelligence and starts generating new hypotheses for us to investigate.